course: dictionary
lesson: 14015
title: Magically
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Magically is an adverb. An adverb tells us how something happens. It means by magic, as in fairy tales. «The frog magically turned into a prince.» Nobody can really do that, so the clue tells you the change happened by magic.
explain: The second meaning is more everyday. It means in a wonderful, surprising way, as if by magic. «The lost key magically appeared under my pillow.» We do not know how the key got there, and it seems almost like magic.
explain: People also use magically as a joke. «The biscuits magically vanished from the tin» usually means someone ate them, and everyone knows it. To check your meaning, swap in «by magic» or «as if by magic». If the sentence still makes sense, you have it right.
fact: Magically is an adverb: it tells us how something happens.
fact: «Magically» means by magic, as in fairy tales.
fact: «Magically» can also mean in a wonderful, surprising way, as if by magic.
fact: People often use magically jokingly when they do not know how something happened, or when they are teasing.
fact: A good check is to swap in «as if by magic» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The frog magically turned into a prince.» What does magically mean here? | Very slowly and carefully | By accident, with no reason at all | In a loud voice | *By magic
exercise: «The lost key magically appeared under my pillow.» What does magically suggest? | The key was too heavy to lift | *In a surprising way, as if by magic | The key was lost for a long time
exercise: Which word in «The dragon magically flew over the sea» tells how the dragon flew? | sea | *magically | flew | dragon
test: «The rain stopped and a rainbow magically filled the sky.» What does magically mean here? | Under the ground | In a slow, boring way | *In a wonderful, surprising way
test: Ella says magically is only for fairy tales. Is she right? | Yes, it is never used in ordinary talk | Yes, but only at Christmas | *No, people also use it for everyday surprises, as in «The bus magically arrived on time»
exam: Which sentence uses magically in a surprising, everyday way? | *«The socks magically vanished from my drawer.» | «The dog ran slowly across the park.» | «I walked to school with my brother.» | «The cake was baked by my nan in an hour.»
